        "heading": "2.2 ENERGY CONSUMPTION-AWARENESS",
        "text": "Resource-constrained NAS for obtaining efficient architectures has been explored mainly by optimising the total number of floating point operations (FPOs) (Tan & Le, 2019a). Optimising for FPOs, however, might not be entirely indicative of the efficiency of models (Henderson et al., 2020). It has been reported that models with fewer FPOs have bottleneck operations that can consume the bulk of the training time (Howard et al., 2017), and some models with high FPOs have lower inference time (Jeon & Kim, 2018).\nThe energy consumption during the training of a model, on the other hand, encapsulates facets of architecture efficiency that are not entirely taken into consideration when using standard resource constraints such as FPOs, computational time and the number of parameters. Energy consumption accounts for both hardware and software variations in the experimental set-ups. To foster a new direction for NAS to find more efficient architectures, we use energy consumption as the additional objective along with standard performance measures."
